### What is the problem?

Given K sorted linked lists, merge them into one sorted linked list and return its head. The challenge is to do this efficiently, exploiting the fact that each list is already sorted rather than ignoring it.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

import java.util.PriorityQueue;
import java.util.Comparator;

public class MergeKSortedLists {

    static class ListNode {
        int val;
        ListNode next;
        ListNode(int val) { this.val = val; }
    }

    public static ListNode mergeKLists(ListNode[] lists) {
        PriorityQueue<ListNode> heap = new PriorityQueue<>(
            Comparator.comparingInt(n -> n.val));

        for (ListNode head : lists) {
            if (head != null) heap.offer(head);
        }

        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0);
        ListNode tail = dummy;

        while (!heap.isEmpty()) {
            ListNode smallest = heap.poll();
            tail.next = smallest;
            tail = smallest;
            if (smallest.next != null) {
                heap.offer(smallest.next);
            }
        }
        return dummy.next;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // List 1: 10000 -> 30000 -> 50000
        ListNode l1 = new ListNode(10000);
        l1.next = new ListNode(30000);
        l1.next.next = new ListNode(50000);

        // List 2: 20000 -> 40000
        ListNode l2 = new ListNode(20000);
        l2.next = new ListNode(40000);

        // List 3: 15000 -> 25000 -> 60000
        ListNode l3 = new ListNode(15000);
        l3.next = new ListNode(25000);
        l3.next.next = new ListNode(60000);

        ListNode result = mergeKLists(new ListNode[]{l1, l2, l3});
        System.out.print("Merged: ");
        while (result != null) {
            System.out.print(result.val + (result.next != null ? " -> " : ""));
            result = result.next;
        }
        System.out.println();
    }
}
```

```text
Merged: 10000 -> 15000 -> 20000 -> 25000 -> 30000 -> 40000 -> 50000 -> 60000
```

### Why does a min-heap of K fronts work?

At any point, the smallest unconsumed element across all K lists must be at the front of one of the lists (since each list is sorted). The heap holds these K fronts and gives us the global minimum in O(log K) time. After removing the minimum, we push its successor to maintain one candidate per list.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

public class WhyHeapWorks {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("K sorted lists, each in ascending order.");
        System.out.println("The global minimum must be at one of the K fronts.");
        System.out.println("A min-heap of K fronts finds it in O(log K).");
        System.out.println("After removing it, push its successor: still K fronts.");
    }
}
```

```text
K sorted lists, each in ascending order.
The global minimum must be at one of the K fronts.
A min-heap of K fronts finds it in O(log K).
After removing it, push its successor: still K fronts.
```

### How does the naive approach compare?

The naive approach concatenates all K lists into one array of N elements and sorts, giving O(N log N). This ignores the per-list sorted order. The heap approach is O(N log K), which is better when K is much smaller than N.

| Approach | Time | Space | Exploits sorted? |
|---|---|---|---|
| Concatenate + sort | O(N log N) | O(N) | No |
| Min-heap of K | O(N log K) | O(K) | Yes |
| Pairwise merge | O(N log K) | O(1) extra | Yes |

### What is the divide-and-conquer alternative?

Instead of a heap, you can pair up the K lists and merge them two at a time, halving the number of lists each round. After log K rounds, all lists are merged. This also achieves O(N log K) but avoids the heap.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

public class PairwiseMerge {

    static class ListNode {
        int val; ListNode next;
        ListNode(int v) { this.val = v; }
    }

    static ListNode mergeTwo(ListNode a, ListNode b) {
        ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0), tail = dummy;
        while (a != null && b != null) {
            if (a.val <= b.val) { tail.next = a; a = a.next; }
            else { tail.next = b; b = b.next; }
            tail = tail.next;
        }
        tail.next = (a != null) ? a : b;
        return dummy.next;
    }

    static ListNode mergeKLists(ListNode[] lists) {
        if (lists.length == 0) return null;
        int interval = 1;
        while (interval < lists.length) {
            for (int i = 0; i + interval < lists.length; i += 2 * interval) {
                lists[i] = mergeTwo(lists[i], lists[i + interval]);
            }
            interval *= 2;
        }
        return lists[0];
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ListNode l1 = new ListNode(10); l1.next = new ListNode(40);
        ListNode l2 = new ListNode(20); l2.next = new ListNode(50);
        ListNode l3 = new ListNode(30); l3.next = new ListNode(60);
        ListNode result = mergeKLists(new ListNode[]{l1, l2, l3});
        while (result != null) {
            System.out.print(result.val + " ");
            result = result.next;
        }
        System.out.println();
    }
}
```

```text
10 20 30 40 50 60
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

| Metric | Value |
|---|---|
| Time | O(N log K) |
| Space | O(K) for the heap |
| N | Total number of nodes across all lists |
| K | Number of lists |

### How is this used in a banking system?

At the National Bank of India, settlement events arrive from K regional systems, each stream already sorted by timestamp. The clearing engine merges them into one globally time-ordered stream. The min-heap of K heads merges the streams while holding only K fronts in memory, producing the master order in a single pass.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

import java.util.PriorityQueue;
import java.util.Comparator;

public class SettlementMerge {

    record Event(long timestamp, String region, String details) {}

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Simulate 3 regional streams, each sorted by timestamp
        Event[][] regions = {
            {new Event(100, "North", "TXN-A"), new Event(300, "North", "TXN-D")},
            {new Event(150, "South", "TXN-B"), new Event(250, "South", "TXN-C")},
            {new Event(200, "East", "TXN-E"), new Event(400, "East", "TXN-F")}
        };
        int[] indices = new int[regions.length];

        PriorityQueue<int[]> heap = new PriorityQueue<>(
            Comparator.comparingLong(e -> regions[e[0]][e[1]].timestamp()));

        for (int r = 0; r < regions.length; r++) {
            if (regions[r].length > 0) heap.offer(new int[]{r, 0});
        }

        System.out.println("Merged settlement stream:");
        while (!heap.isEmpty()) {
            int[] top = heap.poll();
            Event e = regions[top[0]][top[1]];
            System.out.println("  t=" + e.timestamp() + " " + e.region() + " " + e.details());
            if (top[1] + 1 < regions[top[0]].length) {
                heap.offer(new int[]{top[0], top[1] + 1});
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Merged settlement stream:
  t=100 North TXN-A
  t=150 South TXN-B
  t=200 East TXN-E
  t=250 South TXN-C
  t=300 North TXN-D
  t=400 East TXN-F
```

### Why is a dummy head node useful?

A dummy (sentinel) head node avoids the special case of setting `head = firstNode` vs `tail.next = node`. You always append to `tail.next` and return `dummy.next` at the end.

```java
package com.bank.sort;

public class DummyHeadBenefit {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Without dummy: need if(head==null) check on first append.");
        System.out.println("With dummy: always append to tail.next, return dummy.next.");
        System.out.println("Cleaner code, fewer branches.");
    }
}
```

```text
Without dummy: need if(head==null) check on first append.
With dummy: always append to tail.next, return dummy.next.
Cleaner code, fewer branches.
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Interviewers often ask:
- What if the lists are arrays instead of linked lists? (Same heap approach, track array index.)
- How does this relate to external merge sort? (Same K-way merge step on sorted runs from disk.)
- Can you do it in O(1) extra space? (Pairwise merge uses O(1) extra but O(N log K) time.)
